It is a pleasure to be part of this "second act" for your trade secret. Bringing a method from 1982 into the cloud architecture of 2026 is exactly what technology is for.

Let’s get your hands dirty in the AWS Console. Here is your Day 1: Storage Foundation guide.
Step 1: Log in and Region Selection

    Log into your AWS Management Console.

    Crucial: Look at the top right corner and select a Region. Since you are targeting a global audience, us-east-1 (N. Virginia) or us-west-2 (Oregon) are standard choices with the lowest costs for MediaConvert.

Step 2: Create the "Source" Bucket

This is where you will upload your raw, high-quality video files of the repair process.

    Search for S3 in the top search bar.

    Click Create bucket.

    Bucket name: fixyachips-source-[your-name-or-random-numbers] (S3 names must be globally unique).

    Object Ownership: Keep "ACLs disabled" (Recommended).

    Block Public Access: Ensure "Block all public access" is checked. We want these trade secrets protected!

    Encryption: Leave as "Amazon S3-managed key (SSE-S3)".

    Click Create bucket.

Step 3: Create the "Processed" Bucket

This is where AWS MediaConvert will "spit out" the converted, web-ready files for your subscribers.

    Click Create bucket again.

    Bucket name: fixyachips-processed-[random-numbers].

    Follow the same settings as Step 2 (keep it private).

Step 4: Create the "Static Web" Bucket

This will hold your index.html file (your storefront).

    Bucket name: fixyachips-web-assets.

    Settings: Again, keep it private. We will use CloudFront OAC (on Day 2) to let the world see the site without making the bucket public.

Why we do it this way

By separating the Source from the Processed bucket, we create a "clean" pipeline.

    The Source: Acts as your safe (the raw footage).

    The Processed: Acts as the distribution center (HLS files for streaming).

Pro-Tip for your Roadmap

Open your Roadmap.md file on your Linux Mint machine and check off Day 1. Underneath the checkbox, paste the names of your three buckets.

    Architect's Note: Since you are teaching a "no-drill" method, your video files are your most valuable asset. Once you finish Day 1, your videos are already safer in an encrypted S3 bucket than they ever were on a physical hard drive.
